{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794611147,"nonce":"OzA3lDrPRq8UtbPhYCzDQHQktvo=","title":"A CSS keyframe animation of transform alone is moved by rows, not baked into a texture","message":"When bound nodes are moved by rows (the default), a CSS keyframe animation of translate, scale and rotate on a node with no transform or transform-origin of its own is carried by a motion binding. The node is painted once and each frame writes a value. Before this, the same animation was baked into a texture per node (and held that texture) or, with text inside, painted in full on every frame.\n\nHow it works: apply_css_driven_props drives a binding from the animation's current values (AnimatedValue::drive_playing, which reads as animating until released, and does not wake the loop), the compositor and the CSS cache check ask css_active_off_rows so a carried animation is not treated as CSS work, and the binding is released when the animation stops. It is the same technique as FLIP (1379b4f). A tree that has not been rendered yet now has motion promotion off, as the default configuration does; otherwise the first frame wrote the animation into the node's props, which made it uncarriable.\n\nMeasured, 140 cards under a transform keyframe animation, release build on the dev Mac:\n- Headless, no text: 1.9 ms a frame on rows against 11.2 ms baked.\n- Real window, text in the cards, same frame rate: steady-state CPU 0.043 cores at 30 fps (0.057 baked) and 0.077 at 60 fps (0.090 baked). Uncapped it draws 120 fps against 44 fps for 0.125 cores against 0.104.\n- Real window peak memory footprint: 91 MB on rows, 911 MB baked.\nMethod: tools/blinc-bench css_window_check with /usr/bin/time -l, CPU as the slope between 6 s and 18 s runs; animation_paths.rs (what_a_css_animated_frame_costs, how_much_memory_css_animated_cards_hold).\n\nChecked against the full paint: a carried card matches it to the pixel; a carried label stays within 2 px of the card's centre and keeps 94 to 100% of the full paint's ink, but its edges differ from the full paint by up to 79 levels on about 480 px in the rotated frames (a sub-pixel difference in where the glyphs land). I have not found why.\n\nNot done, each its own issue: opacity animations (carried they cost more than baked, since every bound opacity is an offscreen group), CSS transitions, and the mobile runners (efa5528).","files":null}]}